00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.369
in the House and Senate that they th th th
there seems to be a sort of a, you scratch

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:28.770
our back, we'll scratch yours sort of crony
capitalist.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.190
I mean, it's like the worst of the worst in
an area that a lot of people don't even know,

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.710
uh, is taking place in a sense.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:40.830
Yeah, I mean, so it gets into this whole,
the whole area of tax filing and sort of,

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:45.760
you know, the annual ritual that we all go
through doing our taxes.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:52.369
Um, it turns out that in a lot of other countries,
uh, no one really has to spend hours filling

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:55.070
out forms and doing their taxes.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:01.570
And the fundamental reason for that is that
the tax agency often already has your data.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:05.160
So if you're, if you're getting paid every
couple of weeks and taxes are being automatically

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.610
taken out of your paycheck, right?

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.560
Then the IRS, the IRS already has all those
numbers.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.650
And so in many other countries, they have
what's sometimes called return free tax filing,

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.540
which is basically, uh, the IRS takes all
the numbers that already have prepopulates

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.780
your return.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.210
Um, and then you sort of just look at it and
click okay if it's all good.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.470
And so the question becomes, well, why don't
we have that here?

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:33.560
And the answer, or at least part of the answer
is that turbo tax, uh, the company that makes

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:39.640
it in to it, um, and the rest of the industry
have been lobbying against, uh, that kind

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.630
of system for at least 20 years at this point.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.630
Yeah.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.870
And so, so the, the way this works is pretty
simple, which is if you make money off of

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.640
a process that is perceived as too complicated
to do or actually is too complicated to do

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.860
without these pieces of software, you're far
more likely to buy one of these pieces of

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.770
software and pay, pay the filing fees.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.730
Now, what a lot of people don't know, and
correct me if I'm wrong, I'm going off the

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.050
top of my head.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:10.550
If you make under some certain amount of money
a year, it might be in the 60 thousands federal

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:13.040
filing at ready is free.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.120
And those companies like TurboTax provide
that free federal filing, but they just don't

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.870
make it too easy to figure that out for yourself.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.959
[inaudible] you're, you're exactly right.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:29.970
So basically what what has happened is a turbo
tax and the rest of the industry have made

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.260
this deal with the IRS.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.080
It's called free file.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.330
Um, and the deal says, uh, two things.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:41.790
First it says that the IRS can't create its
own system so the government can offer you

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:47.760
essentially its own turbo tax or they can't,
uh, or you know, create something like that.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.960
And in exchange, the industry is supposed
to provide free filing.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:58.819
Two people this year was making under $66,000,
which turns out to be actually the majority

00:03:58.819 --> 00:03:59.819
of the country.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:00.819
Yeah.

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:05.819
And then what you experience and when you
actually go to choose the version of turbo

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:10.220
tax or whatever that you're going to use,
it doesn't, uh, it's not made evident that

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.730
there is such an option.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:16.329
And instead you're immediately sort of upsold
with if you want to maximize your deductions,

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.419
take advantage of deduction finder.

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:22.819
If you have any schedule C income or did any
sort of work on the side, you should get the

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:26.710
small, uh, small and home business edition
or something along those lines.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:31.839
Now, what's important to understand is that
that does not account for the state.

00:04:31.839 --> 00:04:37.199
Where, correct me if I'm wrong, those costs
are actually regulated at the state level

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:40.039
and are not free on the basis of income.

00:04:40.039 --> 00:04:43.330
Um, it's so it's a whole sort of complicated
patchwork.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:47.849
And so the at, at the heart of it is, as I
mentioned, there's this program called free

00:04:47.849 --> 00:04:54.860
file that actually literally most Americans,
70% should be able to use, uh, to file at

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.659
least federally for free.

00:04:56.659 --> 00:05:03.319
Um, but what, what TurboTax intuit, the company
that makes TurboTax has done is create a separate

00:05:03.319 --> 00:05:06.949
product that very confusingly is called just
free.

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:09.770
So free file and free are different things.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:18.849
Um, and uh, almost no one actually finds the
sort of truly free a free file version because

00:05:18.849 --> 00:05:21.789
intuit advertises this other version.

00:05:21.789 --> 00:05:27.689
And if you end up in this quote unquote free
edition, in fact, many people will end up

00:05:27.689 --> 00:05:29.960
having to pay a hundred, 200 bucks.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:34.119
Uh, even though if they had just gone to a
different website, it would have actually

00:05:34.119 --> 00:05:35.309
truly been free.

00:05:35.309 --> 00:05:39.939
And this is essentially a significant part
of the company's business model at this point.

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:44.899
What's the argument that the government makes
for new capping itself on this?

00:05:44.899 --> 00:05:50.039
In other words, we can kind of try to infer
what's going on behind the scenes, but what

00:05:50.039 --> 00:05:54.729
is this sort of public facing explanation
that the government says we believe in restricting

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:56.499
ourselves in this way?

00:05:56.499 --> 00:05:58.849
You know, that's a really good question.

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:05.020
I've actually never heard anyone at the IRS
could dress that aspect of it.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.319
I would be very interested to, to hear their
answer.

00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:08.969
And we've asked them about this.

00:06:08.969 --> 00:06:17.089
Um, what they will say more generally is that
they support this program because it's a public

00:06:17.089 --> 00:06:22.449
private partnership where the government,
instead of having to spend money on creating

00:06:22.449 --> 00:06:27.979
its own system and employing people, uh, will
sort of lean on this already existing private

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:33.589
industry that's, that's offering this product,
uh, for free, which sounds good.

00:06:33.589 --> 00:06:37.509
[inaudible] might be a good thing, uh, if
people were actually using it.

00:06:37.509 --> 00:06:44.990
But in fact something like two or maybe 3%
of Americans who are eligible for this free

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:46.780
fall program actually use it.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:51.270
So virtually no one who's eligible actually
uses it.

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:57.520
Um, and we've never really been able to get
any one of the government to comment on, on,

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:03.059
you know, why those numbers are so low and
sort of what their thinking is in the program.

00:07:03.059 --> 00:07:07.059
This seems to me like one of those things
that has really negatively affected by, uh,

